+**Reviewer's verdict (human, product owner): reject.**
+
+The computed recommendation had no P1 finding. Fix H1 and the nine minor findings (H2–H10) in this pull request, then re-raise; the five small findings (H11–H15) are filed as one chore.
+
+Layer 1: component linter — nothing in changed files; play linter — start-change, propose-change, merge-change pass; 16 shared test files pass; standards check — no findings.
+
+- **H1 (P2, major)** `core/components/plays/merge-change/scripts/preflight.py`:122 — The new issue rule returns no issue for the plan's own branch (plan/<id>/base). The script says so in its own comment. Yet the new pause rule sends exactly that branch through commit-change, propose-change, check-change and merge-change, and every one of them keys its run folder, session stamp, PR issue reference and stop-condition path on the issue the resolver returns. resolve_target.py in the same change gives that branch the issue number of the plan (kind plan, issue 717), so the two scripts disagree. I ran both on plan/717/base: preflight.py returns issue null, resolve_target.py returns issue 717. *Impact:* Pausing a plan is the headline path of this change, and on it the pre-flight facts carry no issue. The run folder path would be built from an empty value, and the PR reference rule (the PR must name its issue) has nothing to name. The runs for this very PR worked only because the agents guessed the plan number, which is the inference the scripts are there to remove. Fix: let preflight.py take the plan number from plan/<id>/base (the same rule resolve_target.py uses) and re-stamp all copies. *Basis:* standards/rules/pipeline-position.md (end plays find their issue from the branch); docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, section 3 (resolve the facts by script, not by inference)
+- **H2 (P3, minor)** `docs/standards/harness-standard.md`:88 — Principle 8 is rewritten (a plan lands on main whenever it pauses, instead of only when every issue is closed) and the same rule is added to .garura/user-provided/managing-work.md and to the ICE of all three plays. The branch adds no ADR (no file under docs/adr/ changed), and no ADR on origin/main records the plan branch rule at all (searched docs/adr for plan branch wording: none). *Impact:* The decision of 2026-10-28 is held only in a working note and in the standard's own new text, so a later reader cannot see who decided it, what it replaced, or which plays and ICE it amends. The rule itself is not in question; the missing record is. *Basis:* docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, 'How the standard is kept' (a change to the standard goes through record-decision as an ADR); docs/adr/055-installed-parts-read-only-installed-files.md (an earlier amendment of this standard carried its own ADR)
+- **H3 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/propose-change/SKILL.md`:10 — The ICE Intent paragraph changed in all three plays, and start-change's SKILL.md lead and description follow it. For propose-change and merge-change they do not. merge-change still opens with "switch to main and pull the latest, and delete the feature branch" (lead at line 10 and the frontmatter description at line 3), propose-change's lead has no plan target, its pre-flight row still says "On a feature branch (not main)", its optional approval step still shows "base = main", and merge-change's closing report still says the change "has landed on main". A rebuild from the new ICE would not write these lines, which is the sign of a patched file rather than a compiled one, even though the fingerprints match. *Impact:* The skill list and the approval text tell people the old behaviour. An issue in a plan lands on its plan branch, but the text a person approves says main. The approval step is switched off by config today, so the harm is a misleading description, not a wrong merge. *Basis:* docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, section 4 (the compiled part matches its intent); CLAUDE.md at origin/main, 'Play Pipeline Rules' (an intent change is made in the ICE and compiled, never hand-edited)
+- **H4 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/start-change/scripts/setup_branch.py`:67 — The plan block only runs when the issue's own branch does not exist yet (line 67). On a resume, where the issue branch already exists, base_sha stays at the origin/main tip and plan_branch_created is false. But eval SE-2 says that for an issue in a plan, base_sha equals the plan branch tip. On a resume that holds only when the plan branch happens to sit level with main. *Impact:* Scenario S3 (resume) for an issue in a plan can fail SE-2 although nothing is wrong, or a reader trusting base_sha as "where this branch was cut" is told the wrong commit. Resume is a path the play promises works (C6). *Basis:* review playbook review/harness (every step eval must hold for the script it checks); docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, section 5 (the check must be able to fail for the right reason)
+- **H5 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/create-play/SKILL.md`:255 — The compiler's list of job scripts to copy into a play (setup_branch.py, submit_pr.py, read_merge_state.py, merge_pr.py, fetch_pr_context.py, post_verdict.py) does not name the two new scripts, resolve_plan.py and resolve_target.py. The file is not changed by this branch. *Impact:* The new scripts live in the three plays and in create-play/references, but the instructions that stamp scripts into a play never mention them. A rebuild of a play, or a new play built later, may not carry them, and the plan wiring would quietly drop out. *Basis:* CLAUDE.md at origin/main, 'Durability rule for non-intent edits' (shared scaffolding is emitted by the compiler); core/components/plays/create-play/SKILL.md at origin/main, 'Code-host scripts' ('a rebuild brings them back')
+- **H6 (P3, minor)** `core/tools/lint-components/lib/rules/shared-scripts.js`:10 — This change edits 27 stamped copies of preflight.py at once and adds two stamped copies of resolve_target.py and one of resolve_plan.py. The shared-scripts rule checks only lint_grounding.py and model_names.py, so nothing in the linters would catch a copy that drifts later. Today all copies are identical (checked by hand). *Impact:* The next hand edit of one play's preflight.py or resolve script slips through the linter, and plays then disagree about how an issue is read from a branch. This is the gap that H1 would have been caught by. *Basis:* CLAUDE.md at origin/main, 'Durability rule for non-intent edits' (shared scaffolding is linted); core/tools/lint-components/lib/rules/shared-scripts.js at origin/main (only lint_grounding.py and model_names.py are held byte-identical)
+- **H7 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/propose-change/reference/ice.md`:86 — propose-change has one new scenario, S5, for an issue in a plan. Its constraint C2 and failure F10 also say a plan's own branch (plan/<id>/base) must open against main, and that is the path a paused plan takes. No scenario or eval raises that branch. *Impact:* The pause-and-land path is proved only on the merge side (merge-change S6). A plan branch raised against the wrong base, or raised with no issue (see H1), would pass every propose-change check in the play. *Basis:* docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, section 5 (build the eval first; a scenario for each promised path)
+- **H8 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/propose-change/SKILL.md`:304 — Recovery F10 is marked autonomous and says "change the PR's base on the host to the target". No script does this. When a PR already exists, submit_pr.py only edits the title and body (line 88, a direct gh call) and never the base, so a re-run leaves the wrong base in place and the run closes unmet on on_target. *Impact:* The recovery named for a wrong-base PR cannot run by itself, so the run halts with an instruction nobody automates. Either give submit_pr.py a retarget step through the adapter or mark the recovery as a person's. *Basis:* docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, section 3 (mechanical work is a script); the play's own recovery contract (one recovery per failure, handoff stated)
+- **H9 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/merge-change/scripts/merge_pr.py`:162 — The close-issue and capability-close steps run only on the fresh-merge path (lines 196 to 206). The already-merged path (line 162) records on_target and ends, so when a first run merged the PR and then stopped before closing the issue, a re-run never closes it. *Impact:* An issue that landed on its plan branch can stay open for good, and the plan tree then shows it as open work. Failure F9 names "a landed issue left open" and sends it to a person, so the gap is known, but the cheap repair (do it on re-run) is missing. *Basis:* Constraint C5 of merge-change at origin/main (a re-run on a merged PR is a clean no-op) read with the new C9 (an issue that lands on its plan branch is closed by the merge script)
+- **H10 (P3, minor)** `core/components/plays/start-change/scripts/setup_branch.py`:69 — A plan branch counts as existing if it exists locally or on origin (_branch_exists). After a pause the merge script deletes it on origin and in the clone that merged. A second clone that still holds the old local plan/<id>/base is treated as live: the script merges main into that stale branch instead of cutting a new one, and pushes it back to origin. If the plan was squash merged, the old commits conflict with main. *Impact:* On a second machine the restart rule does not hold. The run either stops on a conflict or recreates the deleted plan branch with old history. Check origin first, and cut fresh when origin has no such branch. *Basis:* .garura/user-provided/managing-work.md on this branch states 'When the plan starts again, a new branch is cut from main' (the rule under review); start-change ICE C9 says the branch is cut only when it does not exist
+- **H11 (P4, small)** `core/components/plays/start-change/SKILL.md`:58 — The pre-flight row "Read plans.base-path" is not among the facts preflight.py prints (it prints worktree, stm paths and others, but no plans path). The model has to read the key from config by itself, while the sibling worktree flag comes from the script. *Impact:* A small drift risk: the folder resolve_plan.py reads is chosen by the model, not by the resolver. If the key is missing in an older project config, the default is used and the issue silently gets a feature branch. *Basis:* docs/standards/harness-standard.md at origin/main, section 3; core/components/plays/start-change/SKILL.md at origin/main (worktree flag is resolved by preflight.py, 'not by inference')
+- **H12 (P4, small)** `core/components/plays/propose-change/scripts/submit_pr.py`:106 — When create-pr succeeds but the follow-up lookup finds no number, the record is built with base set to the requested base (lines 101 to 104). Line 106 then compares that value with itself, so on_target is always true on this branch of the code. *Impact:* D3 cannot catch a wrong base on that path. The path is rare, and the normal path reads the base from the host. *Basis:* review playbook review/harness (an eval must be able to fail)
